PARS = [
("Surface fire spread is described", """Surface fire spread is described by Rothermel-type rate-of-spread models driven by fuel, moisture, wind, and slope {7;27;28}. These models are implemented in operational tools such as FlamMap {8} and in cellular simulators such as Cell2Fire {29}. The simulation core of VODA uses a model of the same family, as described in Section III. Decision support built on these models ranges from strategic risk assessment {4;9} and multi-criteria allocation {10;16;30} to the integrated WFDSS platform {11}, whose field use has been studied extensively {12;13;14}. In all of them the decision logic is authored offline and the intervention menu is fixed. Fire management economics judges a response by the sum of two terms, the cost of the resources committed and the loss that still occurs {3;31}. The decision cost of VODA follows the same structure. Its physical terms measure the loss, and its response terms measure the cost of the orders."""),

("B. Closed and Open Decision Spaces", """B. Fuzzy Reasoning Over Concepts"""),

("Decision analysis has long assumed", """Operational assessments arrive as graded, linguistic judgments, such as a high fire threat or a low suppression feasibility. Fuzzy set theory gives these judgments a formal counterpart, partial membership in a linguistic term {52}. A fuzzy rule base encodes domain knowledge as IF-THEN rules over such terms, and fuzzy inference turns the degree to which the rules fire into a numeric output. In the Takagi-Sugeno (TS) form the consequent of a rule is a constant or a function rather than a fuzzy set, and the output is the firing-strength weighted average of the consequents {39}. Evolving TS models extend this form by creating and tuning rules online within the existing vocabulary {40}. VODA uses zero-order TS inference, in which every consequent is a constant intensity. The choice is architectural. Candidates are judged inside shadow forecasts that run many times per incident, so the costly centroid integration of other fuzzy forms is avoided. Moreover, the outputs are intensity commands, and adaptation needs numeric consequents with predictable effect while the antecedents stay linguistic. Applying inference directly over a rich observation vector is combinatorially infeasible, since ten features with five terms each span nearly ten million antecedent combinations. A conceptual middle layer reduces this to five decision concepts. Each concept answers exactly once one of the questions operators ask {3;23}. Thus, the antecedent space shrinks to 3125 combinations, only the active subset is instantiated, and the concepts double as the vocabulary in which every recommendation is explained."""),

("C. Learned and Generative Components", """C. Closed and Open Decision Spaces"""),

("Recent wildfire and disaster systems", """Decision analysis has long assumed that the set of alternatives is given before evaluation begins {32;33}. Arbel and Tong {18} identified option generation as a neglected phase that precedes choice. Colorni and Tsoukias {19;34} formalized the construction of the alternative set as a recursion of decision problems. Hofstede {20} observed that planning systems which hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not adopted. Cognitive evidence points the same way. Option generation in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval, and generating more options raises the quality of the best option while it lowers the mean quality of the set {35}. Operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from enumerated menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation, and the generated set must be filtered by a strict, external evaluation."""),

("D. Fuzzy Reasoning Over Concepts", """D. Learned and Generative Components in Disaster Decision Support"""),

("Operational assessments arrive as graded, linguistic judgments, whose", """Recent wildfire and disaster systems have added learned and generative components, but the intervention space has stayed closed or unchecked. Dubey and Dubey {44} couple a Transformer forecaster with fifteen expert rules that map predicted risk to four fixed response levels. Chandra et al. {45} draw suggested actions from a predefined ontology and check them for consistency only. Cheng et al. {46} let LLMs weight a fixed hierarchy of indicators. Gerolimos et al. {47} call for open-world learning in disaster management, but the openness concerns the recognition of unseen event classes while the response stays a mapping to familiar actions. At the other end, multi-agent LLMs produce free-text response plans whose hallucinations are contained by retrieval grounding, output schemas, or post-hoc grading {48;49}. Cui et al. {50} conclude that external verification through physics-based simulation is required. Nevertheless, they confine their own agent to a fixed tool library. WildfireKR {51} is the closest neighbour. It is an LLM agent that automates calibrated spread simulation, but it produces suppression-free envelopes and leaves intervention design to the analyst. The remaining gap is a system whose intervention space is open at run time, in which every new intervention is built from effects a physical model can execute and admission is decided by that model rather than by the proposer, a schema, or the decision maker alone."""),
]
