"""Three kinds of mechanism have been used to open a decision space. The first keeps the model fixed and searches it for solutions that differ in structure, not only in value, as in modeling to generate alternatives {21} and in the generator-evaluator separation of active decision support {22}. The second builds alternatives by combining modules under compatibility constraints {17;36}. The third adds new actions to the action model while the system runs, as in open-world planning and action discovery {37;38}. In all three cases the new alternatives are made of parts the system already understands. Hence, an open decision space is defined here as an open composition over a closed and verified basis. Two design rules follow. Generation must be cheap to reject, because most compositions do not change the outcome, and the evaluator must be independent of the generator. The mechanisms that change a rule base at run time follow the same pattern. Evolving TS models tune and add rules within the existing vocabulary {39;40}, value-based selectors learn when a structural change is needed {41}, and generative models draft rules outside the current structure {42}. VODA places the first inside the closed layer and the last inside the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {43}.""",
